window.ADAPTIVE_EXAM_TRANSCRIBED_CAMBRIDGE_PAPERS=[{"id":"exam-01","examNumber":1,"label":"01 · CAMBRIDGE · B2 First Trainer 3 · Test 1","source":{"type":"Cambridge","label":"01 · CAMBRIDGE · B2 First Trainer 3 · Test 1","detail":"Cambridge B2 First Trainer 3 · source scan and official key stored in the Drive master bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Multiple-choice cloze","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-01-part-1.webp","segments":["Many people all over the world are (0) _____ concerned about the environment. As a result, they are gradually ",{"n":1,"answers":["adopting"],"answer":"adopting","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["using","adopting","welcoming","accepting"]}," more environmentally friendly lifestyles. One of the ways in which they are ",{"n":2,"answers":["taking"],"answer":"taking","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["doing","taking","having","setting"]}," action is by changing the way they eat. Around a billion tonnes of food are thrown away each year and food waste often ",{"n":3,"answers":["generates"],"answer":"generates","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["generates","breeds","results","leads"]}," methane, a harmful greenhouse gas.\n\nNo individual is personally ",{"n":4,"answers":["responsible"],"answer":"responsible","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["blamed","obliged","responsible","guilty"]}," for changing the whole world, but many people are altering their diets in order to ",{"n":5,"answers":["contribute"],"answer":"contribute","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["donate","support","contribute","add"]}," in a small way. Many try to eat what's ",{"n":6,"answers":["widely"],"answer":"widely","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["highly","extremely","strongly","widely"]}," available locally rather than a lot of imported food, or eat less meat and more plants, especially beans and greens.\n\nIt isn't necessary to become completely vegetarian or vegan all of a ",{"n":7,"answers":["sudden"],"answer":"sudden","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["sudden","rush","flash","moment"]},". Simply having a meat-free day once a week can ",{"n":8,"answers":["make"],"answer":"make","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["give","put","bring","make"]}," a difference. Planning meals in advance and using up leftover food also helps to reduce unnecessary waste."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Open cloze","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Read the text and think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-01-part-2.webp","segments":["Some people are concerned (0) _____ the impact of computer and video games. It is of course true that some players become addicted to them and once that has happened, it may be hard to know ",{"n":9,"answers":["what"],"answer":"what","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," to do about it.\n\nThere are also many positive aspects of gaming, however. ",{"n":10,"answers":["as","like"],"answer":"as","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," many gamers will tell you, it has become a means for many to communicate regularly ",{"n":11,"answers":["with"],"answer":"with","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," those they care most about. This is true for people of all ages, not just those in ",{"n":12,"answers":["their"],"answer":"their","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," teens or twenties.\n\nNowadays, almost ",{"n":13,"answers":["everyone","everybody"],"answer":"everyone","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," has a family member or friend living so far away that it is impossible to spend time with them ",{"n":14,"answers":["in"],"answer":"in","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," person. Gaming is a great way of bringing people together, ",{"n":15,"answers":["without"],"answer":"without","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," anyone needing to leave their own home.\n\nGaming provides a chance to catch up on news, exchange gossip and simply relax. Moreover, it costs relatively ",{"n":16,"answers":["little"],"answer":"little","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," compared to travelling great distances to meet up."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Word formation","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-01-part-3.webp","segments":["Everyone knows the world's oceans are full of (0) _____ creatures. Nevertheless, it's always exciting to think that there are still plenty of ",{"n":17,"answers":["discoveries"],"answer":"discoveries","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"DISCOVER"}," to be made. Recently, a group of ",{"n":18,"answers":["scientists"],"answer":"scientists","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"SCIENCE"}," set off on a deep-sea expedition aiming to explore underwater mountain ranges off the coast of Chile. They wanted to increase their ",{"n":19,"answers":["knowledge"],"answer":"knowledge","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"KNOW"}," of the diverse sea life there.\n\nWhat they found was totally ",{"n":20,"answers":["unbelievable"],"answer":"unbelievable","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"BELIEVE"},"! In less than a month, the expedition recorded 100 deep-sea animals that they had never seen before, ",{"n":21,"answers":["including"],"answer":"including","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"INCLUDE"}," species of corals and sponges. ",{"n":22,"answers":["additionally"],"answer":"additionally","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ADDITION"},", they recorded new types of octopus and jellyfish, as well as many other fantastic creatures that are of interest to researchers.\n\nThe remote area explored was ",{"n":23,"answers":["roughly"],"answer":"roughly","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ROUGH"}," the size of Italy. The team had started with high ",{"n":24,"answers":["expectations"],"answer":"expectations","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"EXPECT"}," because they knew that this place was likely to contain some previously unknown species. However, the number they found was far greater than they had ever imagined it would be."]}}},{"id":"exam-02","examNumber":2,"label":"02 · CAMBRIDGE · B2 First Trainer 3 · Test 2","source":{"type":"Cambridge","label":"02 · CAMBRIDGE · B2 First Trainer 3 · Test 2","detail":"Cambridge B2 First Trainer 3 · source scan and official key stored in the Drive master bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Multiple-choice cloze","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-02-part-1.webp","segments":["Houseplant sales have (0) _____ recently as more and more people have been creating their own 'indoor gardens'. Having plants in a room is a relatively easy and inexpensive ",{"n":1,"answers":["means"],"answer":"means","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["technique","step","process","means"]}," of making it more attractive and welcoming, and many people enjoy ",{"n":2,"answers":["showing"],"answer":"showing","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["pointing","setting","showing","giving"]}," off the latest additions to their collection on social media.\n\nIf the idea of indoor gardening ",{"n":3,"answers":["appeals"],"answer":"appeals","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["draws","attracts","interests","appeals"]}," to you, but you're not sure whether or not to ",{"n":4,"answers":["go"],"answer":"go","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["go","fall","care","head"]}," for it, all you need to do is look online. Plant influencers with over a million followers share videos and provide tips on how to get started and how to ",{"n":5,"answers":["make"],"answer":"make","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["get","make","take","have"]}," the most of small spaces.\n\nSome of the most popular houseplants include snake plants, spider plants and peace lilies. Swiss cheese plants, with leaves of up to a metre long, are also favourites. Many people end up absolutely ",{"n":6,"answers":["devoted"],"answer":"devoted","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["passionate","devoted","loyal","emotional"]}," to their gardens and say they lose ",{"n":7,"answers":["track"],"answer":"track","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["feeling","sense","track","idea"]}," of time while looking after them, something they appreciate in their ",{"n":8,"answers":["otherwise"],"answer":"otherwise","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["otherwise","apart","alternatively","elsewhere"]}," busy lives."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Open cloze","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Read the text and think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-02-part-2.webp","segments":["Solar panels are seen more (0) _____ more often on the roofs of buildings in many parts of the world. Solar batteries – known as cells – provide plenty of power as ",{"n":9,"answers":["long"],"answer":"long","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," as there is enough sun. Smaller solar cells are sometimes used in wearable technology such as smartwatches. For a long time, however, using them in smart clothing or other soft products has proved ",{"n":10,"answers":["too"],"answer":"too","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," difficult to be worth doing on ",{"n":11,"answers":["a"],"answer":"a","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," large scale.\n\nHowever, this may soon change because solar cells have now ",{"n":12,"answers":["been"],"answer":"been","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," invented which can bend and stretch in different directions without breaking. The solar cells are ",{"n":13,"answers":["not"],"answer":"not","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," only easy to put into clothing, but also still ",{"n":14,"answers":["able"],"answer":"able","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," to work efficiently while being stretched and strained during use.\n\nThe new cells can be stretched to as much as 40% larger than their original size, so ",{"n":15,"answers":["it"],"answer":"it","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," is hoped that engineers and designers will soon come ",{"n":16,"answers":["up"],"answer":"up","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," with many interesting ways of using them."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Word formation","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-02-part-3.webp","segments":["Have you ever heard the term 'five-a-day' in (0) _____ with exercise? Studies show that even small amounts of regular movement can be ",{"n":17,"answers":["beneficial"],"answer":"beneficial","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"BENEFIT"}," to health. In fact, just three and a half minutes per day of ",{"n":18,"answers":["energetic"],"answer":"energetic","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ENERGY"}," activity like stair-climbing will do you good. So, getting your fitness five-a-day doesn't mean big workouts of over an hour in ",{"n":19,"answers":["length"],"answer":"length","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"LONG"}," – it's micro-breaks of movement that can easily fit into your ",{"n":20,"answers":["lifestyle"],"answer":"lifestyle","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"LIFE"},".\n\nAnd ",{"n":21,"answers":["unlike"],"answer":"unlike","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"LIKE"}," some expensive sports activities, this kind of 'exercise snacking' costs nothing. For example, you can start walking while on the phone or spend a few minutes a day dancing. You'll be able to change your ",{"n":22,"answers":["behaviour","behaviours"],"answer":"behaviour","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"BEHAVE"}," more easily if you can maintain your new habits, so make use of activities you already do on a ",{"n":23,"answers":["daily"],"answer":"daily","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"DAY"}," basis. Brushing your teeth is a mindless activity you do ",{"n":24,"answers":["automatically"],"answer":"automatically","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"AUTOMATIC"}," every day, so why not use that time to get a mini workout in? And remember, doing something is always better than doing nothing!"]}}},{"id":"exam-03","examNumber":3,"label":"03 · CAMBRIDGE · B2 First Trainer 3 · Test 3","source":{"type":"Cambridge","label":"03 · CAMBRIDGE · B2 First Trainer 3 · Test 3","detail":"Cambridge B2 First Trainer 3 · source scan and official key stored in the Drive master bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Multiple-choice cloze","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-03-part-1.webp","segments":["How can we (0) _____ ourselves to feel happy? Studies have found that our attitude to life can ",{"n":1,"answers":["play"],"answer":"play","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["take","give","play","hold"]}," an important part. Taking a more optimistic ",{"n":2,"answers":["approach"],"answer":"approach","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["belief","approach","thought","strategy"]}," and learning to feel grateful for the good things in our lives can provide ",{"n":3,"answers":["protection"],"answer":"protection","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["protection","security","defence","safety"]}," against negative emotions. It can also increase our happiness ",{"n":4,"answers":["levels"],"answer":"levels","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["levels","degrees","amounts","extents"]},".\n\nListing things at the end of every day that we feel grateful for can help us think more positively and feel better. This can be done in a ",{"n":5,"answers":["number"],"answer":"number","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["volume","collection","set","number"]}," of ways, for example by ",{"n":6,"answers":["keeping"],"answer":"keeping","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["keeping","filling","maintaining","storing"]}," a daily gratitude diary, which can be written by hand or on your phone. Another quick and easy habit is simply writing down three things that ",{"n":7,"answers":["went"],"answer":"went","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["were","did","went","looked"]}," well every day and thinking about what was good about them. You can also tell someone what you are grateful for that day or send a thank-you message.\n\nThis might all sound rather simple, but it does seem that feeling grateful is ",{"n":8,"answers":["associated"],"answer":"associated","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["combined","associated","attached","considered"]}," with increased social well-being."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Open cloze","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Read the text and think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-03-part-2.webp","segments":["In 2022, the French baguette, a long, thin loaf of bread eaten by many French people (0) _____ day, was added to a list of culturally protected traditions by UNESCO, the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization. The list has traditions from over 130 countries on it. ",{"n":9,"answers":["apart"],"answer":"apart","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," from food, it also includes activities and events such ",{"n":10,"answers":["as"],"answer":"as","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," dances and festivals. UNESCO said that ",{"n":11,"answers":["by"],"answer":"by","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," including the bread on the list, it was protecting the traditional method ",{"n":12,"answers":["of"],"answer":"of","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," baking baguettes.\n\nThe origins of the stick-shaped bread are unknown. Some say the French emperor Napoleon Bonaparte invented the longer shape in ",{"n":13,"answers":["order"],"answer":"order","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," to make it easier for people to carry. Others say the bread was in ",{"n":14,"answers":["fact"],"answer":"fact","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," invented in the 1830s by an Austrian baker called August Zang. ",{"n":15,"answers":["what"],"answer":"what","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," is certain is that the bread has been at ",{"n":16,"answers":["the"],"answer":"the","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," centre of French life for more than a century and around 16 million baguettes a day are still produced in France."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Word formation","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-03-part-3.webp","segments":["Have you ever been to a sand-sculpture festival? In Weymouth, on the south coast of England, visitors to SandWorld can see amazing work by some of the world's (0) _____ sand sculptors – artists who make sculptures out of sand.\n\nThe ",{"n":17,"answers":["breathtaking"],"answer":"breathtaking","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"BREATH"}," sculptures can be seen in spring, summer and autumn. They're so ",{"n":18,"answers":["beautifully"],"answer":"beautifully","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"BEAUTY"}," detailed, it's hard to believe they're just sand and water. Science-fiction adventures have provided ",{"n":19,"answers":["inspiration"],"answer":"inspiration","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"INSPIRE"}," for many of them and there are dinosaurs, too. New displays are added throughout the season and many visitors get the chance to observe the sculptors creating their ",{"n":20,"answers":["impressive"],"answer":"impressive","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"IMPRESS"}," works of art.\n\nVisitors can also create their own sand sculptures. There's plenty of advice available for anyone trying the activity out for the first time, so it's a fun and ",{"n":21,"answers":["educational"],"answer":"educational","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"EDUCATE"}," day out for people of all ages. It's also easily ",{"n":22,"answers":["accessible"],"answer":"accessible","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ACCESS"}," for people in wheelchairs. There's a charge for ",{"n":23,"answers":["admission"],"answer":"admission","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ADMIT"}," to the attraction, with group tickets available. Alternatively, a season ticket will allow you an ",{"n":24,"answers":["unlimited"],"answer":"unlimited","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"LIMIT"}," number of visits throughout the summer."]}}},{"id":"exam-04","examNumber":4,"label":"04 · CAMBRIDGE · B2 First Trainer 3 · Test 4","source":{"type":"Cambridge","label":"04 · CAMBRIDGE · B2 First Trainer 3 · Test 4","detail":"Cambridge B2 First Trainer 3 · source scan and official key stored in the Drive master bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Multiple-choice cloze","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-04-part-1.webp","segments":["There are few (0) _____ statistics about the number of shoes manufactured every year for the world's eight billion humans. We do know, however, that modern shoes are ",{"n":1,"answers":["among"],"answer":"among","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["inside","among","between","including"]}," the hardest items to produce in an ecologically friendly way. That's because most shoes are ",{"n":2,"answers":["composed"],"answer":"composed","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["composed","created","combined","constructed"]}," of a mixture of artificial cloth, rubber, plastic and metal that's too complex to recycle.\n\nA UK shoe company has ",{"n":3,"answers":["come"],"answer":"come","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["thought","made","come","dreamt"]}," up with a design for the world's first 3D printed, made-to-measure shoes which can be composted – turned into soil – at the end of their life. The shoes may ",{"n":4,"answers":["strike"],"answer":"strike","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["knock","hit","punch","strike"]}," some people as rather strange. As a result, not everyone will therefore regard them as the ",{"n":5,"answers":["future"],"answer":"future","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["progress","future","prospect","fate"]}," of fashionable footwear. They are also ",{"n":6,"answers":["extremely"],"answer":"extremely","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["totally","absolutely","completely","extremely"]}," expensive. Their inventors, however, hope this will be the next huge ",{"n":7,"answers":["breakthrough"],"answer":"breakthrough","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["breakthrough","movement","boost","addition"]}," in eco-footwear.\n\nThe shoes are produced based on in-store foot scans and then take 30 hours to print. Once they are ",{"n":8,"answers":["worn"],"answer":"worn","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["used","worn","tired","spent"]}," out, the shoes can be returned and composted."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Open cloze","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Read the text and think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-04-part-2.webp","segments":["The Republic of Estonia is a very beautiful country (0) _____ the Baltic Sea in Europe. Its capital city, Tallinn, is a popular tourist destination. However, there are also plenty of reasons ",{"n":9,"answers":["to"],"answer":"to","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," explore the country's second largest city, Tartu.\n\nTo start ",{"n":10,"answers":["with"],"answer":"with","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."},", Tartu is the oldest city in the Baltic states and has one of the oldest universities ",{"n":11,"answers":["in"],"answer":"in","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," the world, founded in the 1600s. Around ",{"n":12,"answers":["a","one"],"answer":"a","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," quarter of the city's population are students. There's plenty to do ",{"n":13,"answers":["all"],"answer":"all","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," year round and visitors love the film festival ",{"n":14,"answers":["which","that"],"answer":"which","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," is held every summer, during which movies are shown on the Baltics' largest outdoor movie screen.\n\nThere are over 20 museums, including the popular Tartu Toy Museum. Visitors should also ",{"n":15,"answers":["have"],"answer":"have","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," the opportunity to see the Tartu Leaning House. It ",{"n":16,"answers":["was"],"answer":"was","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," built in the 1700s next to Tartu's ancient city walls and leans over even more than the famous Leaning Tower of Pisa in Italy."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Word formation","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-04-part-3.webp","segments":["The American writer Edith Wharton (1862–1937) is best known nowadays as a (0) _____, although her first novel was not published until she was 40. Born into a ",{"n":17,"answers":["wealthy"],"answer":"wealthy","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"WEALTH"}," New York family, she travelled in Europe with her parents as a child, ",{"n":18,"answers":["consequently"],"answer":"consequently","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"CONSEQUENCE"}," becoming fluent in French, German and Italian. She wrote stories from early ",{"n":19,"answers":["childhood"],"answer":"childhood","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"CHILD"},", attempting her first novel aged just 11. It attracted such fierce ",{"n":20,"answers":["criticism"],"answer":"criticism","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"CRITIC"}," from her mother, however, that she started writing poetry instead.\n\nHer first published work was a translation of a German poem. She was 15 years old. Her family insisted that it should appear under another name, because writing was considered ",{"n":21,"answers":["unsuitable"],"answer":"unsuitable","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"SUIT"}," as an occupation for a rich American woman. Nevertheless, she refused to be ",{"n":22,"answers":["discouraged"],"answer":"discouraged","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"COURAGE"}," and eventually became an extraordinarily ",{"n":23,"answers":["productive"],"answer":"productive","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"PRODUCE"}," writer. By the time of her ",{"n":24,"answers":["death"],"answer":"death","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"DIE"},", she had published 15 novels, seven novellas and 85 short stories in addition to her poetry, as well as books on design, travel, literature and culture."]}}},{"id":"exam-05","examNumber":5,"label":"05 · CAMBRIDGE · B2 First Trainer 3 · Test 5","source":{"type":"Cambridge","label":"05 · CAMBRIDGE · B2 First Trainer 3 · Test 5","detail":"Cambridge B2 First Trainer 3 · source scan and official key stored in the Drive master bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Multiple-choice cloze","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-05-part-1.webp","segments":["Thought to be between 3,500 and 5,000 years old, Stonehenge (0) _____ around 1.5 million visitors a year. Yet the purpose of this circle of giant stones in south-west England ",{"n":1,"answers":["remains"],"answer":"remains","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["continues","stays","remains","keeps"]}," a mystery. How it was made is also a matter for ",{"n":2,"answers":["debate"],"answer":"debate","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["debate","question","doubt","wonder"]}," – theories about how the 25-ton stones were transported to the area over great distances are many and ",{"n":3,"answers":["varied"],"answer":"varied","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["varied","wide","mixed","broad"]},".\n\nStonehenge was not the only circular 'henge' monument built in ancient Britain, ",{"n":4,"answers":["though"],"answer":"though","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["besides","meanwhile","indeed","though"]},". Another was Seahenge, a circle of oak columns made 4,000 years ago and rediscovered on an English beach in 1998. It is believed that like Stonehenge, Seahenge was ",{"n":5,"answers":["designed"],"answer":"designed","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["measured","designed","prepared","controlled"]}," to be in a specific position in ",{"n":6,"answers":["relation"],"answer":"relation","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["reference","connection","comparison","relation"]}," to the sun on certain days every year. This is thought to have been extremely ",{"n":7,"answers":["significant"],"answer":"significant","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["major","significant","principal","essential"]}," in the lives and beliefs of ancient societies. In particular, sunrise on the longest day of the year and sunset on the shortest day were ",{"n":8,"answers":["key"],"answer":"key","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["key","main","great","chief"]}," dates for ancient farmers."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Open cloze","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Read the text and think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-05-part-2.webp","segments":["Nowadays, there are apps for nearly anything you can think (0) _____. So it should come as ",{"n":9,"answers":["no"],"answer":"no","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," surprise that if you're interested in giving your creativity ",{"n":10,"answers":["a"],"answer":"a","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," boost, there are apps for that, too.\n\nSome of these apps use sound to stimulate your imagination. ",{"n":11,"answers":["for"],"answer":"for","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," instance, you may hear a sequence of sounds that inspires you to make up a story to go with it. Others send you a short text or image every morning which aims to wake up your brain and give you new things ",{"n":12,"answers":["to"],"answer":"to","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," think about.\n\nMany puzzle apps can help your brain to think ",{"n":13,"answers":["in"],"answer":"in","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," different ways, potentially unlocking creative solutions to problems you ",{"n":14,"answers":["have"],"answer":"have","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," been struggling with for a while. And there are apps that help you organise your ideas, with tools ",{"n":15,"answers":["such"],"answer":"such","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," as mind maps.\n\nAnd last ",{"n":16,"answers":["but"],"answer":"but","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," not least, there are all the apps that encourage you to doodle or draw something – another way of unlocking your brain power!"]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Word formation","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-05-part-3.webp","segments":["Mary Anderson (1866–1953) was a US businesswoman who worked in (0) _____ different fields throughout her life. She made a major ",{"n":17,"answers":["contribution"],"answer":"contribution","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"CONTRIBUTE"}," to transport history by inventing the ",{"n":18,"answers":["original"],"answer":"original","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ORIGIN"}," version of a windscreen wiper.\n\nAnderson was in a streetcar – or tram – in 1902 when she observed that the driver kept the windscreen open, even though the weather was ",{"n":19,"answers":["freezing"],"answer":"freezing","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"FREEZE"},". That was because it was too hard to keep the windscreen clear of snow and thus keep the road ahead ",{"n":20,"answers":["visible"],"answer":"visible","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"VISION"}," while the streetcar was moving. Anderson thought of a solution to this problem and employed a ",{"n":21,"answers":["designer"],"answer":"designer","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"DESIGN"}," to prepare a drawing of a hand-operated system that could be used from inside the vehicle.\n\nSimilar devices had previously been created by others, but Anderson's version proved to be the first one that actually worked. Her ",{"n":22,"answers":["effective","efficient"],"answer":"effective","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"EFFECT"}," design has, of course, now been ",{"n":23,"answers":["replaced"],"answer":"replaced","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"PLACE"}," by more modern ones, but the basic idea remains the same and her concept made driving ",{"n":24,"answers":["considerably"],"answer":"considerably","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"CONSIDER"}," safer."]}}},{"id":"exam-06","examNumber":6,"label":"06 · CAMBRIDGE · B2 First Trainer 3 · Test 6","source":{"type":"Cambridge","label":"06 · CAMBRIDGE · B2 First Trainer 3 · Test 6","detail":"Cambridge B2 First Trainer 3 · source scan and official key stored in the Drive master bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Multiple-choice cloze","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-06-part-1.webp","segments":["It's an unfortunate (0) _____ of evolution that vegetables are so good for us, but aren't immediately tasty to all of us. Humans adapted over thousands of years to enjoy the taste of higher-energy foods because the most immediate risk was not eating enough, rather than ",{"n":1,"answers":["harm"],"answer":"harm","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["trouble","harm","suffering","ruin"]}," to long-term health. Vegetables aren't particularly high-energy, but they're ",{"n":2,"answers":["packed"],"answer":"packed","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["packed","complete","full","crowded"]}," with healthy substances, including some called 'bioactives'.\n\nBioactives are part of the ",{"n":3,"answers":["reason"],"answer":"reason","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["explanation","motive","reason","cause"]}," vegetables taste bitter. Humans developed a dislike of bitter tastes to protect themselves from poisons and possibly as protection from over-eating one ",{"n":4,"answers":["single"],"answer":"single","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["single","alone","absolute","only"]}," plant food. So in a ",{"n":5,"answers":["way"],"answer":"way","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["manner","truth","fact","way"]},", plant foods can taste like poison.\n\nFor some people, this bitter taste is particularly strong, while others ",{"n":6,"answers":["barely"],"answer":"barely","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["barely","fairly","slightly","virtually"]}," notice it. However, if you're in the former category, the good ",{"n":7,"answers":["thought"],"answer":"thought","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["idea","news","side","thought"]}," is that we can all train ourselves to enjoy vegetables. If we ",{"n":8,"answers":["keep"],"answer":"keep","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["ensure","stay","recall","keep"]}," including them in our diet, our brain gradually learns that they aren't poisonous and they actually start to taste less bitter."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Open cloze","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Read the text and think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-06-part-2.webp","segments":["The term 'Impressionist' was first used (0) _____ an insult in response to an exhibition of new paintings in Paris in 1874 by a diverse group of painters which included Monet, Renoir, Pissarro and Degas.\n\nModern life and ordinary people having ",{"n":9,"answers":["a"],"answer":"a","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," good time were popular subjects with many Impressionists. They painted theatres, cafés and popular countryside resorts. Traditionally in French painting, poorer people ",{"n":10,"answers":["had"],"answer":"had","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," rarely been shown as individuals or people ",{"n":11,"answers":["who","that"],"answer":"who","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," should be taken seriously.\n\nEven ",{"n":12,"answers":["more"],"answer":"more","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," significant to the Impressionists was an interest ",{"n":13,"answers":["in"],"answer":"in","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," how the human mind processes what it sees. When we look at a landscape or a crowd of people, we do ",{"n":14,"answers":["not"],"answer":"not","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," instantly see every face or leaf in detail, but as a mass of colour and light. Impressionists tried to express this experience in their paintings.\n\nToday, Impressionist paintings are ",{"n":15,"answers":["some"],"answer":"some","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," of the best known and best loved in the world. It can be hard to imagine ",{"n":16,"answers":["how"],"answer":"how","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," revolutionary the movement was once considered."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Word formation","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-06-part-3.webp","segments":["Paddleboarding is a relatively (0) _____ watersport in which a person stands or kneels on a board and uses a paddle – like a canoe paddle, but longer – to move themselves forwards. Modern stand-up paddleboarding is ",{"n":17,"answers":["originally"],"answer":"originally","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ORIGIN"}," from Hawaii and was adopted by west-coast American ",{"n":18,"answers":["surfers"],"answer":"surfers","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"SURF"}," in the early 2000s. It has been growing in ",{"n":19,"answers":["popularity"],"answer":"popularity","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"POPULAR"}," ever since.\n\nUnlike surfing, which requires waves, it is suitable for people who want to explore coastlines or rivers. Many of the more ",{"n":20,"answers":["reasonably"],"answer":"reasonably","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"REASONABLE"}," priced paddleboards can be filled with air at the water's edge, making them easy to transport.\n\nSome paddleboards are longer than others, ",{"n":21,"answers":["depending"],"answer":"depending","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"DEPEND"}," on how fast people want to go, their ",{"n":22,"answers":["height"],"answer":"height","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"HIGH"}," and where they want to paddleboard. The boards also vary in ",{"n":23,"answers":["width"],"answer":"width","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"WIDE"},".\n\nThere are places where you're allowed to go along certain waterways, but it's ",{"n":24,"answers":["illegal"],"answer":"illegal","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"LEGAL"}," to go along others. So, if you decide to go paddleboarding and don't want to get into trouble, always check before you set off!"]}}},{"id":"exam-07","examNumber":7,"label":"07 · CAMBRIDGE · First for Schools 2 · Test 5","source":{"type":"Cambridge","label":"07 · CAMBRIDGE · First for Schools 2 · Test 5","detail":"Cambridge First for Schools 2 · source scan and official key stored in the Drive master bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Multiple-choice cloze","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-07-part-1.webp","segments":["Dreams are (0) _____ stories and pictures that our brains create when we're asleep. It is ",{"n":1,"answers":["common"],"answer":"common","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["typical","routine","ordinary","common"]}," knowledge that the majority of dreams take place while we're in deep sleep, and our eyes begin to move quickly under our eyelids. This is called Rapid Eye Movement, or REM. Although dreams can ",{"n":2,"answers":["occur"],"answer":"occur","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["exist","result","occur","reveal"]}," at any point during the night, REM dreams are typically the most memorable and realistic.\n\nSome experts believe that dreams are a way to ",{"n":3,"answers":["process"],"answer":"process","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["deal","process","cope","arrange"]}," all the events of the day helping us to make ",{"n":4,"answers":["sense"],"answer":"sense","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["reason","logic","point","sense"]}," of what has happened. Others suggest that dreams are the result of our brains trying to solve ",{"n":5,"answers":["specific"],"answer":"specific","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["definite","specific","absolute","determined"]}," problems. Dreams may also be our brain's attempt to interpret what is going on around us while we sleep. For example, the sound of the radio may be included in the content of our dreams. ",{"n":6,"answers":["Alternatively"],"answer":"Alternatively","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["Particularly","Especially","Alternatively","Consequently"]},", dreaming could be a sort of clean-up operation, refreshing our minds in preparation for the next day. However, there are also experts who ",{"n":7,"answers":["claim"],"answer":"claim","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["claim","realise","wonder","expect"]}," that dreams have no real ",{"n":8,"answers":["purpose"],"answer":"purpose","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["aim","purpose","intention","design"]}," at all."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Open cloze","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Read the text and think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-07-part-2.webp","segments":["Do you help (0) _____ with jobs that need doing around the house? If you are anything ",{"n":9,"answers":["like"],"answer":"like","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," me, you do, but you hate making your bed! Well, there's good news, thanks ",{"n":10,"answers":["to"],"answer":"to","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," a Spanish company which has designed a bed that makes ",{"n":11,"answers":["itself"],"answer":"itself","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."},". You can press a button, which automatically makes the bed after you get out of it. Your bed will be made in just 50 seconds, leaving you time to do other things. As the inventor explained: 'Many people hate leaving home without ",{"n":12,"answers":["having"],"answer":"having","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," made their bed, but they are often in too ",{"n":13,"answers":["much"],"answer":"much","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," of a hurry to bother. For others, the job is simply ",{"n":14,"answers":["so"],"answer":"so","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," boring that they keep ",{"n":15,"answers":["putting"],"answer":"putting","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," it off until later. An unmade bed can be the cause of arguments between parents and their children.' The design of the bed making machine ",{"n":16,"answers":["is"],"answer":"is","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," being finalised now and the bed should be available for sale in the near future. Good news for lazy people!"]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Word formation","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-07-part-3.webp","segments":["Most famous (0) _____ figures are adults, and yet our world has also been shaped by children and teenagers.\n\nThe German ",{"n":17,"answers":["mathematician"],"answer":"mathematician","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"MATHEMATICS"},", Carl Friedrich Gauss (1777–1855), made the first of several important ",{"n":18,"answers":["discoveries"],"answer":"discoveries","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"DISCOVER"}," while he was still in elementary school. One day, his teacher asked him to add up all the numbers from 1 to 100. To the teacher's great surprise, Carl ",{"n":19,"answers":["produced"],"answer":"produced","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"PRODUCT"}," the answer in seconds. The formula he devised to do this is still used by scientists worldwide to ",{"n":20,"answers":["enable"],"answer":"enable","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ABLE"}," them to come up with ",{"n":21,"answers":["solutions"],"answer":"solutions","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"SOLVE"}," to other maths problems.\n\nYoung people have also contributed to cultural history. In 1816, horror writing was changed forever when, at eighteen, Mary Shelley, the English ",{"n":22,"answers":["novelist"],"answer":"novelist","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"NOVEL"},", began work on Frankenstein. US teenage fiction was changed ",{"n":23,"answers":["dramatically"],"answer":"dramatically","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"DRAMA"}," when 15-year-old S. E. Hinton wrote The Outsiders in 1965. Both these books gained long-lasting popularity, but the writers' ages are rarely mentioned. Now, however, there is far more interest in celebrating the many ",{"n":24,"answers":["achievement","achievements"],"answer":"achievement","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ACHIEVE"}," of young people throughout history."]}}},{"id":"exam-08","examNumber":8,"label":"08 · CAMBRIDGE · First for Schools 2 · Test 6","source":{"type":"Cambridge","label":"08 · CAMBRIDGE · First for Schools 2 · Test 6","detail":"Cambridge First for Schools 2 · source scan and official key stored in the Drive master bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Multiple-choice cloze","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-08-part-1.webp","segments":["Lacrosse is a team game which (0) _____ in mid-America, probably during the 12th century. Long-handled sticks with small nets on the end are used to catch, carry and throw a rubber ball. Players score by shooting the ball into the opposing team's goal. In 1994, lacrosse was ",{"n":1,"answers":["declared"],"answer":"declared","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["declared","claimed","announced","revealed"]}," Canada's national summer sport. This traditional Native American game sometimes ",{"n":2,"answers":["lasted"],"answer":"lasted","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["took","lasted","spent","passed"]}," for days. Teams ",{"n":3,"answers":["consisting"],"answer":"consisting","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["consisting","combining","containing","composing"]}," of anything from 100 to 1,000 men played on a field that ",{"n":4,"answers":["stretched"],"answer":"stretched","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["expanded","increased","ranged","stretched"]}," for many kilometres. ",{"n":5,"answers":["Early"],"answer":"Early","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["First","Initial","Early","Prior"]}," lacrosse balls were made of deerskin, clay and stone.\n\nIn 1856, Dr Beers, a Canadian dentist, ",{"n":6,"answers":["founded"],"answer":"founded","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["installed","founded","developed","realised"]}," the Montreal Lacrosse Club. He shortened the game and reduced the number of players to twelve per team. Until the 1930s, all lacrosse was played on outdoor fields. When an indoor ",{"n":7,"answers":["version"],"answer":"version","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["kind","category","version","type"]}," of the game, called Box Lacrosse, was created, it soon became the most common form of the sport in Canada, partly due to the severe winter weather that ",{"n":8,"answers":["limits"],"answer":"limits","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["limits","excludes","controls","reserves"]}," opportunities for outdoor play."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Open cloze","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Read the text and think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-08-part-2.webp","segments":["Ears do (0) _____ than hear. They keep you balanced, as ",{"n":9,"answers":["well"],"answer":"well","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."},". Inside the inner ear, three small loops, or semi-circular canals, can be found. They are filled ",{"n":10,"answers":["with"],"answer":"with","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," liquid and have thousands of microscopic hairs lining them.\n\nWhen you move your head, the liquid in the canals also moves. This causes the tiny hairs ",{"n":11,"answers":["to"],"answer":"to","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," move too, sending a nerve message telling the brain what position your head is in. Almost instantly, your brain sends messages to your muscles, and this ",{"n":12,"answers":["makes"],"answer":"makes","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," it possible for you to keep your balance.\n\nIf you've just been spinning around, the liquid in the canals keeps moving ",{"n":13,"answers":["despite"],"answer":"despite","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," the fact that you have actually stopped turning. ",{"n":14,"answers":["as"],"answer":"as","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," a result, the hairs inside the canals sense movement and that is why you might feel dizzy – your brain is getting two different messages and is confused about the position of your head. Once the fluid ",{"n":15,"answers":["has"],"answer":"has","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," finally stopped moving, your brain gets ",{"n":16,"answers":["the"],"answer":"the","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," right message and you regain your balance."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Word formation","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-08-part-3.webp","segments":["The Baobab tree is an (0) _____ tree that grows in low-lying areas of Africa and Australia. When the Baobab drops its leaves, its branches have the ",{"n":17,"answers":["appearance"],"answer":"appearance","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"APPEAR"}," of roots sticking up into the air, as if it had been planted upside-down. Baobabs range in ",{"n":18,"answers":["height"],"answer":"height","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"HIGH"}," from five to twenty metres, and there's evidence from carbon dating that they may live to be 3,000 years old.\n\nTheir trunks are smooth and shiny and are often hollow. One ancient Baobab in Zimbabwe is so ",{"n":19,"answers":["massive"],"answer":"massive","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"MASS"}," that up to forty people can shelter in the empty space inside it. This space has been used for a variety of purposes, including shops, bus shelters or simply ",{"n":20,"answers":["storage"],"answer":"storage","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"STORE"}," space.\n\nBaobabs are almost ",{"n":21,"answers":["impossible"],"answer":"impossible","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"POSSIBLE"}," to kill, and when they do die, they decay from the inside and collapse ",{"n":22,"answers":["unexpectedly"],"answer":"unexpectedly","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"EXPECTED"}," leaving only a heap of fibres behind. That's the reason for the traditional ",{"n":23,"answers":["belief"],"answer":"belief","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"BELIEVE"}," that they don't actually die, but simply vanish. It's hardly ",{"n":24,"answers":["surprising"],"answer":"surprising","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"SURPRISE"}," they're sometimes called magic trees."]}}},{"id":"exam-09","examNumber":9,"label":"09 · CAMBRIDGE · First for Schools 2 · Test 7","source":{"type":"Cambridge","label":"09 · CAMBRIDGE · First for Schools 2 · Test 7","detail":"Cambridge First for Schools 2 · source scan and official key stored in the Drive master bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Multiple-choice cloze","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-09-part-1.webp","segments":["To many people, winter (0) _____ shorter days and long, dark nights. This ",{"n":1,"answers":["certainly"],"answer":"certainly","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["surely","obviously","clearly","certainly"]}," used to be the case in Rjukan, a small town in Norway, which is surrounded by steep hills that cut it ",{"n":2,"answers":["off"],"answer":"off","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["off","out","away","apart"]}," from direct sunlight for six months each year. But now, the town authorities have come up with a clever way of ",{"n":3,"answers":["getting"],"answer":"getting","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["producing","creating","getting","making"]}," light into the town: giant mirrors situated on top of the hills and controlled by computers are programmed to follow the path of the sun and reflect beams of light into the town centre.\n\nThe idea was ",{"n":4,"answers":["put"],"answer":"put","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["held","come","brought","put"]}," forward over 100 years ago, but it's only now that technological developments have made it possible. Many residents ",{"n":5,"answers":["objected"],"answer":"objected","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["objected","argued","disagreed","refused"]}," to the proposal at first, largely because they thought it was a waste of money, but now there's a really positive ",{"n":6,"answers":["response"],"answer":"response","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["feedback","response","answer","comment"]}," to the mirrors in the town. People sit in the centre ",{"n":7,"answers":["soaking"],"answer":"soaking","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["sponging","absorbing","soaking","mopping"]}," up the sunlight and say that it has really ",{"n":8,"answers":["enhanced"],"answer":"enhanced","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["inflated","enhanced","supplemented","extended"]}," their quality of life."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Open cloze","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Read the text and think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-09-part-2.webp","segments":["Would you like your next walk to be (0) _____ adventure? Then ",{"n":9,"answers":["why"],"answer":"why","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," not try a high-tech treasure hunt known as 'geocaching'? A geocache is a small container hidden outdoors that is located ",{"n":10,"answers":["with"],"answer":"with","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," the help of a map or a GPS app, which can be downloaded onto most mobile phones. To get started, just log on to a website where you can find out about the geocaches near you.\n\nWhen you ",{"n":11,"answers":["come"],"answer":"come","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," across one of the hidden boxes there'll be a record book ",{"n":12,"answers":["for"],"answer":"for","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," you to leave a message in. The box often contains small items of little value that you can take, but the idea ",{"n":13,"answers":["is"],"answer":"is","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," that if you take something, you should leave something in return. This makes the adventure exciting, because you never know ",{"n":14,"answers":["what"],"answer":"what","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," people may have left.\n\nGeocaching is ",{"n":15,"answers":["something"],"answer":"something","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," that all the family can enjoy and there are over 6 million participants worldwide, so join them and ",{"n":16,"answers":["give"],"answer":"give","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," it a go!"]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Word formation","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-09-part-3.webp","segments":["An (0) _____ street painting has been created on a footpath at a university in Nanjing, China. It is 365 metres in ",{"n":17,"answers":["length"],"answer":"length","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"LONG"},", and covers 2,600 square metres. A team of people with ",{"n":18,"answers":["artistic"],"answer":"artistic","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ART"}," talent spent 20 days completing the ",{"n":19,"answers":["spectacular"],"answer":"spectacular","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"SPECTACLE"}," painting. They worked from early morning to late evening, only stopping when it got too dark to tell the colours apart. The size of the painting is ",{"n":20,"answers":["impressive"],"answer":"impressive","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"IMPRESS"},", and so is the fact that when viewed from certain angles, it is three-dimensional in ",{"n":21,"answers":["appearance"],"answer":"appearance","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"APPEAR"},".\n\n",{"n":22,"answers":["unsurprisingly"],"answer":"unsurprisingly","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"SURPRISING"},", the painting has attracted a great deal of attention, and people from all over the world have visited it. It shows Nanjing's architectural landscape, with the river Yangtze flowing through it, and will no doubt provide ",{"n":23,"answers":["inspiration"],"answer":"inspiration","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"INSPIRE"}," for many other street painters. The students at the university have given it an ",{"n":24,"answers":["enthusiastic"],"answer":"enthusiastic","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ENTHUSIASM"}," reception, posting pictures of it online and saying that it is preferable to boring, grey concrete!"]}}},{"id":"exam-10","examNumber":10,"label":"10 · CAMBRIDGE · First for Schools 2 · Test 8","source":{"type":"Cambridge","label":"10 · CAMBRIDGE · First for Schools 2 · Test 8","detail":"Cambridge First for Schools 2 · source scan and official key stored in the Drive master bank."},"parts":{"1":{"title":"Part 1 · Multiple-choice cloze","subtitle":"Questions 1–8","instructions":"Read the text and choose the answer (A, B, C or D) that best fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-10-part-1.webp","segments":["Clothes designers have lots of exciting ideas, but this one is (0) _____ interesting. Imagine if your T-shirt changed colour whenever you put your hand on it, or the moment you stepped into a noisy room! A new fabric has been designed which changes colour when it is ",{"n":1,"answers":["exposed"],"answer":"exposed","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["exposed","given","presented","brought"]}," to touch or sound.\n\nThis unusual reaction ",{"n":2,"answers":["occurs"],"answer":"occurs","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["appears","reveals","occurs","arrives"]}," when ordinary fabric is dyed with special ink, known as thermochromatic ink, which changes colour when the temperature increases or decreases. So, when someone's hand comes into ",{"n":3,"answers":["contact"],"answer":"contact","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["contact","touch","access","connection"]}," with the fabric, its natural heat ",{"n":4,"answers":["reacts"],"answer":"reacts","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["affects","reacts","adapts","responds"]}," with the chemicals in the ink, ",{"n":5,"answers":["resulting"],"answer":"resulting","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["finishing","concluding","leading","resulting"]}," in colourful, but temporary fingerprints.\n\nTo make the fabric change colour when music is playing, special metal wires are woven into it, which are ",{"n":6,"answers":["linked"],"answer":"linked","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["linked","set","combined","put"]}," to a tiny microcomputer. The energy from the sound ",{"n":7,"answers":["causes"],"answer":"causes","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["leads","makes","causes","brings"]}," the wires to heat up, which in ",{"n":8,"answers":["turn"],"answer":"turn","skill":"Part 1","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","options":["place","case","order","turn"]}," change the temperature of the thermochromatic ink. This means that the fabric keeps on changing colour as long as the sound continues."]},"2":{"title":"Part 2 · Open cloze","subtitle":"Questions 9–16","instructions":"Read the text and think of the word which best fits each gap. Use only one word in each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-10-part-2.webp","segments":["Bones belonging (0) _____ a giant penguin that existed around 40 million years ago have recently been unearthed by scientists. They form part of the most complete fossil ever uncovered from the Antarctic. The penguin species, Palaeeudyptes klekowskii, more commonly ",{"n":9,"answers":["known"],"answer":"known","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," as the 'colossus penguin', would have ",{"n":10,"answers":["made"],"answer":"made","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," today's biggest living penguins look small. In fact, they were more or ",{"n":11,"answers":["less"],"answer":"less","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," the same height as the average human, according to researchers.\n\nBy examining those bones, researchers established that the penguins would have stood 2 m tall from toe to beak tip, and weighed as ",{"n":12,"answers":["much"],"answer":"much","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," as 115 kg. In comparison, the tallest and heaviest living penguin, the emperor penguin, stands 1.1 m high and weighs just under 50 kg.\n\n",{"n":13,"answers":["being"],"answer":"being","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," large has its advantages, as bigger penguins can dive underwater to hunt fish ",{"n":14,"answers":["for","over"],"answer":"for","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," significantly longer periods of time compared to smaller species. A penguin ",{"n":15,"answers":["the"],"answer":"the","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," size of Palaeeudyptes klekowskii could have stayed underwater for ",{"n":16,"answers":["up"],"answer":"up","skill":"Part 2","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge."}," to 40 minutes."]},"3":{"title":"Part 3 · Word formation","subtitle":"Questions 17–24","instructions":"Use the word given in capitals to form a word that fits each gap.","sourceImage":"./assets/cambridge/exam-10-part-3.webp","segments":["Like most people, I (0) _____ check the weather forecast before I go out. Knowing what the weather is going to be like helps us plan our ",{"n":17,"answers":["daily"],"answer":"daily","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"DAY"}," activities effectively.\n\nWeather forecasting has a long history. In the past, people recorded changes in the weather to identify patterns which would ",{"n":18,"answers":["enable"],"answer":"enable","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ABLE"}," them to forecast with some ",{"n":19,"answers":["accuracy"],"answer":"accuracy","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ACCURATE"},". They realised, for example, that high, delicate clouds generally announced the ",{"n":20,"answers":["arrival"],"answer":"arrival","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"ARRIVE"}," of good weather.\n\nModern forecasting is more sophisticated, and uses a ",{"n":21,"answers":["combination"],"answer":"combination","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"COMBINE"}," of computer models, monitoring and knowledge of trends. Today's forecasts are based on several different ",{"n":22,"answers":["measurements"],"answer":"measurements","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"MEASURE"},". Rainfall, wind direction, wind speed and air pressure are all recorded. This means that forecasting is becoming increasingly reliable.\n\nNature will always be ",{"n":23,"answers":["unpredictable"],"answer":"unpredictable","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"PREDICT"}," though, so we can never really be sure what will happen. It's difficult to forecast further in advance than five days with much ",{"n":24,"answers":["certainty"],"answer":"certainty","skill":"Part 3","explanation":"Clave oficial Cambridge.","base":"CERTAIN"}," as small changes can have a big impact on the weather."]}}}];
